# 1. First look at the relationship

You have:

```text
Computer
   ↑
   |
   ├── Laptop
   |
   └── Dekstop
```

Because both classes implement `Computer`:

```java
public class Laptop implements Computer
```

and

```java
public class Dekstop implements Computer
```

So both are valid `Computer` objects.

---

# 2. Your `Dev` class

You changed:

```java
private Laptop laptop;
```

to:

```java
private Computer computer;
```

This is a very important change.

You are basically saying:

> "Dev doesn't care whether I get a Laptop or a Desktop. I only need something that behaves like a Computer."

Your `Dev` class:

```java
private Computer computer;
```

and:

```java
public void setComputer(Computer computer) {
    this.computer = computer;
}
```

means `Dev` can accept **any object that implements `Computer`**.

For example:

```java
Laptop laptop = new Laptop();

Dev dev = new Dev();

dev.setComputer(laptop);
```

This works.

And:

```java
Dekstop desktop = new Dekstop();

Dev dev = new Dev();

dev.setComputer(desktop);
```

also works.

Because both satisfy:

```text
Computer
```

---

# 3. But look at your current XML

You have:

```xml
<bean id="dev" class="org.raman.Dev">
    <constructor-arg ref="lap1"/>
</bean>

<bean id="lap1" class="org.raman.Laptop"/>
```

There is a problem here.

Your current `Dev` class, as you showed it, has:

```java
public Dev() {
    System.out.println("Dev constructor");
}
```

but **you did not show a constructor like:**

```java
public Dev(Computer computer) {
    this.computer = computer;
}
```

So this:

```xml
<constructor-arg ref="lap1"/>
```

would require Spring to find a constructor similar to:

```java
Dev(Computer computer)
```

or:

```java
Dev(Laptop laptop)
```

But your current `Dev` only has:

```java
Dev()
```

Therefore, if you want to use the `setComputer()` method you showed, you should use **setter injection**, not constructor injection.

---

# 4. Use setter injection

Your `Dev` already has:

```java
public void setComputer(Computer computer) {
    this.computer = computer;
}
```

So XML should be:

```xml
<bean id="dev" class="org.raman.Dev">
    <property name="computer" ref="lap1"/>
</bean>

<bean id="lap1" class="org.raman.Laptop"/>
```

Now everything matches.

Spring sees:

```xml
<property name="computer" ref="lap1"/>
```

and thinks:

> I need to set the `computer` property of `Dev`.

Spring finds:

```java
setComputer(Computer computer)
```

and calls it.

Conceptually:

```java
Dev dev = new Dev();

Laptop lap1 = new Laptop();

dev.setComputer(lap1);
```

---

# 5. Notice something very important

Your Java variable is:

```java
Computer computer;
```

But the actual object is:

```java
Laptop
```

That's completely valid.

This is normal Java polymorphism:

```java
Computer computer = new Laptop();
```

Similarly, Spring is effectively doing:

```java
Computer computer = new Laptop();
```

inside your `Dev` object.

So:

```text
Dev
 |
 | computer
 ↓
Computer reference
 |
 ↓
Laptop object
```

---

# 6. Now imagine you change Laptop to Desktop

Currently:

```xml
<bean id="lap1" class="org.raman.Laptop"/>
```

Spring creates:

```java
Laptop lap1 = new Laptop();
```

and injects it:

```java
dev.setComputer(lap1);
```

So when you call:

```java
obj.code();
```

your code executes:

```java
computer.compile();
```

The `computer` reference points to a `Laptop`.

Therefore Java calls:

```java
Laptop.compile()
```

and output will be:

```text
Laptop constructor
Compiling in laptop
computer inside of code
```

---

# 7. Now the beauty of using the interface

Suppose you change only XML:

```xml
<bean id="dev" class="org.raman.Dev">
    <property name="computer" ref="desktop"/>
</bean>

<bean id="desktop" class="org.raman.Dekstop"/>
```

You don't change `Dev.java`.

You don't change `App.java`.

You don't change:

```java
computer.compile();
```

Spring now does conceptually:

```java
Dekstop desktop = new Dekstop();

Dev dev = new Dev();

dev.setComputer(desktop);
```

Therefore:

```java
computer.compile();
```

calls:

```java
Dekstop.compile();
```

Output:

```text
Desktop constructor
Compiling in Desktop
computer inside of code
```

---

# 8. This is why we use the interface

Imagine you had written:

```java
private Laptop laptop;
```

Then `Dev` is tightly connected to `Laptop`.

But with:

```java
private Computer computer;
```

`Dev` doesn't care about the implementation.

It can work with:

```text
Laptop
Dekstop
FutureComputer
GamingComputer
Mac
Server
...
```

as long as they implement:

```java
Computer
```

That's **loose coupling**.

---

# 9. The complete Spring flow

With:

```xml
<bean id="dev" class="org.raman.Dev">
    <property name="computer" ref="lap1"/>
</bean>

<bean id="lap1" class="org.raman.Laptop"/>
```

the flow is:

```text
                App.java
                   |
                   ↓
        new ClassPathXmlApplicationContext
                   |
                   ↓
             Spring Container
                   |
                   ↓
              Read beans.xml
                   |
          ┌────────┴─────────┐
          ↓                  ↓
      id="lap1"           id="dev"
      Laptop.class        Dev.class
          ↓                  ↓
     new Laptop()        new Dev()
          ↓                  ↓
          └───────┐          |
                  |          |
                  └──────────┤
                             ↓
                    setComputer(lap1)
                             |
                             ↓
                    Dev.computer
                             |
                             ↓
                       Laptop object
```

Then:

```java
Dev obj = (Dev) context.getBean("dev");
```

gets that already-configured `Dev`.

Then:

```java
obj.code();
```

runs:

```java
computer.compile();
```

Since `computer` contains a `Laptop`:

```text
computer
   ↓
Laptop object
   ↓
compile()
   ↓
"Compiling in laptop"
```

---

# 10. One correction to your current code

If you want **constructor injection with the interface**, you can absolutely do that too.

Add this constructor to `Dev`:

```java
public Dev(Computer computer) {
    this.computer = computer;
    System.out.println("Dev constructor with Computer");
}
```

Then your XML:

```xml
<bean id="dev" class="org.raman.Dev">
    <constructor-arg ref="lap1"/>
</bean>

<bean id="lap1" class="org.raman.Laptop"/>
```

Now Spring effectively does:

```java
Laptop lap1 = new Laptop();

Dev dev = new Dev(lap1);
```

And because:

```java
Laptop implements Computer
```

this is perfectly valid:

```java
Computer computer = lap1;
```

So the two approaches are:

### Setter injection

```xml
<property name="computer" ref="lap1"/>
```

→ Spring calls:

```java
dev.setComputer(lap1);
```

### Constructor injection

```xml
<constructor-arg ref="lap1"/>
```

→ Spring calls:

```java
new Dev(lap1);
```

---

## The key thing I want you to understand

Don't think:

> "`ref=lap1` means put the word `lap1` into `computer`."

Think:

> **"`ref=lap1` means find the actual Spring bean whose ID is `lap1`, and inject that object."**

So:

```xml
ref="lap1"
```

↓

```text
Spring finds bean "lap1"
```

↓

```text
Laptop object
```

↓

```java
setComputer(laptop)
```

↓

```text
Dev
 └── Computer reference → Laptop object
```

And because the reference is of type `Computer`, you can later replace `Laptop` with `Dekstop` **without changing the `Dev` class**. That's the real reason you're learning this interface-based injection example.
